 "caption": "Workflow features of the coded papers against outcome metrics. Round 1: the 233 of 525 eligible earth-science papers published in 2022 that had full text (235) and a coding that passed schema validation; full-text availability leans toward open access and so filters the sample. Coded by claude-opus-5-5 under codebook 1.5.0 on graph v0.4.2; g17 analysis, tools/roadmap/g17_outcomes.py. (a) Spearman rank correlation for each of 245 feature-outcome tests: 49 features (counts per paper from the final-paper sequence; binary features, scored 0/1, kept when present in 10 to n - 10 of all 233 papers) against five outcomes from OpenAlex (retrieved October 2026, about four years after publication): cnp, the citation normalized percentile, the paper's citation count ranked among works of the same OpenAlex subfield, year and type, 0 to 1; FWCI, field-weighted citation impact, citations divided by the mean for those works, 1 = world average; novelty_pair, the rarity of the paper's two leading OpenAlex topics as a pair, -log10(1 + number of 2017-2021 works sharing the pair), higher is rarer; breadth_refs, the share of classified references outside geoscience (OpenAlex fields Earth and Planetary Sciences and Environmental Science, plus audited geoscience topics filed elsewhere); and breadth_citers, the same share for citing works after removing self-citations (only papers with at least 10 such citers, n = 53). novelty_pair and the two breadth measures are not normalized by subfield. A further planned outcome, advance (a blind human rating of the contribution), is not yet measured. Because that filter is applied over all 233 papers, 11 of 49 features fall outside 10 to n - 10 papers in the n = 53 column; those cells are faded and hatched, and their colour rests on 2 to 9 papers with, or without, the feature. Outlined cells have Benjamini-Hochberg q < 0.1 over all 245 tests (two-sided permutation p, 5000 permutations; 2 of the outlined tests sit at the permutation floor p = 1/5001 = 0.0002, so their q is bounded by the number of permutations). V is the Vary or replicate node. (b) The 15 tests with q < 0.1: rho and 95% percentile bootstrap interval (2000 resamples over papers), n = 233 papers each. The cnp and FWCI rows repeat the same features, so the panel holds 12 distinct associations. Borrowed from other fields is the number of concepts, methods, instruments or data from outside Earth science that the coder lists (codebook section 6), and fields borrowed from the number of their distinct source fields; their correlation with breadth_refs is a convergent validity check of the coder (the same thing measured from the text and from the reference list), not a workflow-outcome finding. (c) Rank correlations among the outcomes on their own -1 to 1 colour scale; values written where |rho| >= 0.2; pairs with citers outside geoscience use n = 53, the rest n = 233. cnp and FWCI correlate at 0.99, so their columns in (a) repeat one impact signal rather than giving independent support. Exploratory and descriptive: one model coder, citations about four years after publication, no adjustment for journal or subfield beyond the field normalisation built into cnp and FWCI; no causal reading."
